WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.760
Hi everyone, my name is Erica Cameron and I'm&nbsp;
with the IBM Cloud team. In this video we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.880
going to show you how you can run an application&nbsp;
with IBM Cloud Code Engine starting with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.760
source code instead of a container image. 
 
From the Code Engine "overview" page we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.000
going to choose "Run your source code"... 
 
You'll see that it's pre-populated with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.080
URL to a Github repo - which in this case is the&nbsp;
home to a set of sample workloads that we use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:31.280
demonstrate Code Engine. Let's use that and click&nbsp;
"Start creating" to move to the next step... 
 &nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.040
On this page we can give some more information,&nbsp;
or configuration options, about our application.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.520
To keep it simple we're going to use the&nbsp;
defaults for everything - with one exception.  
 &nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.080
In the "Build details" section we need to&nbsp;
provide it with a bit more details about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.440
to build our image from the source code. Let's&nbsp;
click the "Specify build details" button... 
 &nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.120
Here we can modify any details about&nbsp;
the source that we need to change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.120
In this case we're going to continue to use&nbsp;
the default value.... meaning the git branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.920
is called "master" and the directory in the&nbsp;
repository to find the source code is "hello". 
 &nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.760
Let's click "next" to keep going 
 
On this next panel we can tell Code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.320
Engine how we want to build the image.&nbsp;
Meaning, do we have a "Dockerfile" with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.040
the instructions for how to build it, or&nbsp;
should we use "Cloud Native Buildpacks" that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.800
automatically know how to convert source code in&nbsp;
many popular languages into container images? 
 &nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:33.760
Again, we're going to keep with the defaults&nbsp;
and use the "Dockerfile" that's in the "hello"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.400
directory in the repository. So, let's&nbsp;
keep going by clicking "next" again... 
 &nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.080
On this panel we can tell Code Engine where to&nbsp;
store the image that is going to be built. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.360
Notice that you can choose which Container&nbsp;
Registry to use - in this case it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:52.880
already knows about IBM's container&nbsp;
registry, so we'll just use that. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.800
Then we'll tell it what "namespace" to use&nbsp;
- this is like a folder in the registry so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.480
we can group related images together.  We'll&nbsp;
use the first one in the list, "ce-demo". 
 &nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.000
And finally, we'll give it a Name. We can&nbsp;
either choose the name of an existing image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.120
(and overwrite it), or give it a new name.&nbsp;
We'll give it a new one called "hello" 
 &nbsp;

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.720
And with that we can click “next” and “Create”.  
 &nbsp;

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.960
Notice that the status indicates&nbsp;
that we're doing a build first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:31.040
before the actual deployment of the application. 
 
From our other video about managing Applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.960
this screen probably looks familiar&nbsp;
to you, it's the main configuration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.640
page for our Application and all of the&nbsp;
same options are available to you.  
 &nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.240
The key thing about this flow is that&nbsp;
only the input into the deployment has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.800
changed - meaning we're starting with&nbsp;
"source code" instead of an "image".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.760
Beyond that difference, everything is the same&nbsp;
with respect to how you manage the application. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.120
Now that the build and deploy are done,&nbsp;
as we can see from the "Ready" status,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.840
let's verify it's there by clicking&nbsp;
on the "Application URL" button... 
 &nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.280
And you can see it's just a simple "hello world"&nbsp;
app and it's been deployed to the internet. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.400
Before we wrap this up, it's important to&nbsp;
note that while we deploy an application,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.960
the exact same process could have been used to&nbsp;
deploy a Batch Job. The video about jobs started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.240
with an image, so we could also have started&nbsp;
that workflow with source code as well - just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.800
like we did here. The choice is yours. 
 
That's all the time we have today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.600
but hopefully you found this quick walk-thru of&nbsp;
how to deploy workloads into Code Engine from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.240
source code exciting and I encourage you to look&nbsp;
at our other videos to see more key Code Engine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.000
features. And, of course, we hope that you'll&nbsp;
go visit the Code Engine webpage on ibm.com to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.360
play with it yourself. 
 
Thank you.

